{
  "root_cause": "The xxh32 hash context unserialize validation allowed attacker-controlled XXH32_state_t::memsize values up to 31 (<32). However XXH32_update() assumes memsize is in [0..15] because its internal scratch buffer is only 16 bytes (mem32[4]). When memsize >= 16, (16 - memsize) underflows to a huge size_t and XXH_memcpy() ends up calling memcpy() with an invalid/overlapping range, triggering ASan memcpy-param-overlap and subsequent heap OOB read/corruption.",
  "fix_approach": "1) Hardened PHP’s xxh32 unserialize handler to sanitize invalid memsize values: after successful spec unserialize, if ctx->s.memsize >= 16 the state is reset (XXH32_reset) to a safe initialized state, preventing the invalid invariant from reaching the update path while keeping unserialize non-fatal. 2) Added defense-in-depth in the bundled xxhash.h: XXH32_update() now rejects any state with memsize >= 16 before performing the (16 - memsize) calculation/copy.",
  "files_modified": [
    "ext/hash/hash_xxhash.c",
    "ext/hash/xxhash/xxhash.h"
  ],
  "verification": {
    "build_success": true,
    "repro_success": true,
